{"name":"March Ice","hex":"#d8cdc5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"march","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A formal, rhythmic way of walking, used especially by soldiers, by bands, and in ceremonies.","A journey so walked."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To walk with long, regular strides, as a soldier does.","To cause someone to walk somewhere."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/march"},{"term":"ice","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2/B language code for Icelandic."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Water in frozen (solid) form.","Any frozen volatile chemical, such as ammonia or carbon dioxide."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ice"}],"entries":[{"title":"March","blocks":[{"text":"March is the third month of the year in both the Julian and Gregorian calendars. Its length is 31 days. In the Northern Hemisphere, the meteorological beginning of spring occurs on the first day of March. The March equinox on the 20 or 21 marks the astronomical beginning of spring in the Northern Hemisphere and the beginning of autumn in the Southern Hemisphere, where September is the seasonal equivalent of the Northern Hemisphere's March."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/March","wikidata":"Q110"},{"title":"Ice","blocks":[{"text":"Ice is water that is frozen into a solid state, typically forming at or below temperatures of 0 °C, 32 °F, or 273.15 K. It occurs naturally on Earth, on other planets, in Oort cloud objects, and as interstellar ice. As a naturally occurring crystalline inorganic solid with an ordered structure, ice is considered to be a mineral. Depending on the presence of impurities such as particles of soil or bubbles of air, it can appear transparent or a more or less opaque bluish-white color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg/330px-Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice","wikidata":"Q23392"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Les_Tr%C3%A8s_Riches_Heures_du_duc_de_Berry_mars.jpg/500px-Les_Tr%C3%A8s_Riches_Heures_du_duc_de_Berry_mars.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Les_Tr%C3%A8s_Riches_Heures_du_duc_de_Berry_mars.jpg/500px-Les_Tr%C3%A8s_Riches_Heures_du_duc_de_Berry_mars.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"March, from the Très Riches Heures du Duc de Berry, a book of prayers to be said at canonical hours","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALes_Tr%C3%A8s_Riches_Heures_du_duc_de_Berry_mars.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Daffodil.jpg/500px-Daffodil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Daffodil.jpg/1280px-Daffodil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Daffodil.jpg/500px-Daffodil.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Daffodil, the floral emblem of March","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADaffodil.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/5_Brazilian_aquamarine.JPG/500px-5_Brazilian_aquamarine.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/5_Brazilian_aquamarine.JPG/1280px-5_Brazilian_aquamarine.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/5_Brazilian_aquamarine.JPG/500px-5_Brazilian_aquamarine.JPG?utm_source